  <Section title="Signal to Noise Ratio (SNR)"/>
  <p class="lead">In biomedical engineering and medical instrumentation, measuring physiological phenomena requires capturing extremely delicate electrical potentials directly from the human body. The <strong>Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR)</strong> quantifies the fidelity of an acquired signal by comparing the power of the desired physiological data against unwanted background noise.</p>

  <SubSection title="What is Signal to Noise Ratio (SNR)?" id="What">
      <p>Whether monitoring cardiac activity with an <strong>electrocardiogram (ECG / EKG)</strong>, tracking neural firing with an <strong>electroencephalogram (EEG)</strong>, or measuring muscle exertion with an <strong>electromyogram (EMG)</strong>, biological signals do not exist in a vacuum. They are corrupted by external electromagnetic interference, physiological motion, and intrinsic semiconductor noise.</p>
      
      <p>The <strong>Signal to Noise Ratio (SNR)</strong> is defined as the ratio between the power of the informative target signal and the power of the background interference. A high SNR means the physiological waveform is crisp, clear, and clinically interpretable. Conversely, a low SNR means the target biopotential is obscured or overwhelmed by interference.</p>

