              <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark mb-2">Why SNR Governs Patient Diagnosis</h6>
              <ul class="small text-secondary ps-3 mb-0">
                <li class="mb-2"><strong>Target Signal:</strong> The electrical depolarization waves propagating across the cardiac myocardium. Typical surface amplitude is only <InlineEquation equation="0.5\\text{ mV} - 2.0\\text{ mV}"/>.</li>
                <li class="mb-2"><strong>Noise Floor:</strong> Ambient electrical coupling from the electrical power grid (<InlineEquation equation="60\\text{ Hz}"/>), baseline drift from breathing (<InlineEquation equation="0.2\\text{ Hz}"/>), and skeletal muscle shivering (<InlineEquation equation="20 - 500\\text{ Hz}"/>).</li>
                <li><strong>The Danger:</strong> When noise power approaches or exceeds signal power, critical features like the ST segment (used to diagnose acute heart attacks) are completely obscured.</li>
              </ul>
