  <SubSection title="Mathematical Formulation of SNR" id="MathematicalFormulation">
      <p>SNR can be expressed in two primary ways: as a <strong>dimensionless linear ratio</strong> or on a <strong>logarithmic decibel (<InlineEquation equation="\\text{dB}"/>) scale</strong>.</p>

      <SubSubSection title="1. Linear Power Ratio" id="LinearSNR">
        <p>In linear terms, SNR is the ratio of target signal power (<InlineEquation equation="P_{\\text{signal}}"/>) to unwanted noise power (<InlineEquation equation="P_{\\text{noise}}"/>):</p>
        <DisplayEquation title="Linear SNR" equation="\\text{SNR} = \\frac{P_{\\text{signal}}}{P_{\\text{noise}}} = \\frac{\\sigma_{\\text{signal}}^2}{\\sigma_{\\text{noise}}^2}"/>
        <p>Because electrical power dissipated in a resistor is proportional to the square of voltage (<InlineEquation equation="P = \\frac{V_{\\text{RMS}}^2}{R}"/>), the linear SNR is also equal to the square of the root-mean-square (RMS) amplitude ratio:</p>
        <DisplayEquation title="Linear Amplitude Formulation" equation="\\text{SNR} = \\left( \\frac{A_{\\text{signal}}}{A_{\\text{noise}}} \\right)^2 = \\left( \\frac{V_{\\text{RMS, signal}}}{V_{\\text{RMS, noise}}} \\right)^2"/>
      </SubSubSection>

      <SubSubSection title="2. Logarithmic Decibel (dB) Representation" id="DecibelSNR">
        <p>Because physiological signals and noise can span many orders of magnitude, engineers predominantly express SNR in <strong>decibels (dB)</strong>:</p>
        <DisplayEquation title="Decibel SNR (from Power)" equation="\\text{SNR}_{\\text{dB}} = 10 \\log_{10}\\left( \\frac{P_{\\text{signal}}}{P_{\\text{noise}}} \\right)"/>
        <p>Using logarithmic identities (<InlineEquation equation="\\log(x^2) = 2\\log(x)"/>), when substituting amplitude or voltage into the decibel definition, the multiplier changes from 10 to 20:</p>
        <DisplayEquation title="Decibel SNR (from Amplitude or Voltage)" equation="\\text{SNR}_{\\text{dB}} = 20 \\log_{10}\\left( \\frac{A_{\\text{signal}}}{A_{\\text{noise}}} \\right) = 20 \\log_{10}\\left( \\frac{V_{\\text{RMS, signal}}}{V_{\\text{RMS, noise}}} \\right)"/>
      </SubSubSection>

      <SubSubSection title="Decibel Scale Interpretation" id="DecibelRules">
        <DisplayTable id="snr-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th style="width: 25%;">Decibel Value (<InlineEquation equation="\\text{SNR}_{\\text{dB}}"/>)</th>
              <th style="width: 25%;">Linear Power (<InlineEquation equation="P_{\\text{sig}} / P_{\\text{noise}}"/>)</th>
              <th style="width: 25%;">Linear Amplitude (<InlineEquation equation="A_{\\text{sig}} / A_{\\text{noise}}"/>)</th>
              <th>Physical & Clinical Meaning</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="badge bg-success font-monospace">+40 dB</span></td>
              <td>10,000 : 1</td>
              <td>100 : 1</td>
              <td>Pristine laboratory quality. Noise is imperceptible to human or algorithm.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="badge bg-success font-monospace">+20 dB</span></td>
              <td>100 : 1</td>
              <td>10 : 1</td>
              <td>Diagnostic clinical standard for medical telemetry (ECG, pulse oximetry).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="badge bg-primary font-monospace">+10 dB</span></td>
              <td>10 : 1</td>
              <td>3.16 : 1</td>
              <td>Acceptable for basic heart-rate threshold counting; subtle morphologies degraded.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="badge bg-warning text-dark font-monospace">0 dB</span></td>
              <td>1 : 1</td>
              <td>1 : 1</td>
              <td><strong>Threshold of parity:</strong> Signal power equals noise power. Signal is severely masked.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="badge bg-danger font-monospace">-10 dB</span></td>
              <td>0.1 : 1 (1 : 10)</td>
              <td>0.316 : 1 (1 : 3.16)</td>
              <td>Noise power is 10× greater than signal power. Advanced filtering / averaging required.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="badge bg-danger font-monospace">-20 dB</span></td>
              <td>0.01 : 1 (1 : 100)</td>
              <td>0.1 : 1 (1 : 10)</td>
              <td>Extreme noise. Common in raw evoked potential EEG or deep neural probes before lock-in amplification.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </DisplayTable>
      </SubSubSection>
  </SubSection>

  <SubSection title="Biomedical Noise Sources" id="NoiseSources">
      <p>Biomedical instrumentation designers encounter several distinct categories of noise. Effective filtering requires identifying whether the interference is narrow-band, low-frequency drift, or wideband stochastic noise:</p>

      <div class="row g-3 my-2">
        <div class="col-md-6">
          <div class="card h-100 border-start border-4 border-primary shadow-xs">
            <div class="card-body p-3">
              <h6 class="fw-bold text-primary mb-1">
                <i class="bi bi-lightning-charge me-1"></i> 60 Hz / 50 Hz Powerline Hum
              </h6>
              <p class="small text-muted mb-2"><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Capacitive and inductive coupling between the patient's body (acting as an antenna) and the AC power grid in hospital rooms.</p>
              <p class="small text-secondary mb-0"><strong>Solution:</strong> High Common-Mode Rejection Ratio (CMRR) instrumentation amplifiers, right-leg drive (RLD) active grounding, and sharp 60 Hz analog/digital notch filters.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-md-6">
          <div class="card h-100 border-start border-4 border-warning shadow-xs">
            <div class="card-body p-3">
              <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark mb-1">
                <i class="bi bi-water me-1"></i> Baseline Wander & Motion Artifacts
              </h6>
              <p class="small text-muted mb-2"><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Patient respiration (<InlineEquation equation="0.1 - 0.5\\text{ Hz}"/>) expands the thoracic cavity, modulating electrode contact impedance and half-cell battery potential.</p>
              <p class="small text-secondary mb-0"><strong>Solution:</strong> High-pass analog filtering with cutoff <InlineEquation equation="f_c \\approx 0.05 - 0.5\\text{ Hz}"/> to remove DC offset while preserving low-frequency ST segment data.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-md-6">
          <div class="card h-100 border-start border-4 border-danger shadow-xs">
            <div class="card-body p-3">
              <h6 class="fw-bold text-danger mb-1">
                <i class="bi bi-soundwave me-1"></i> Electromyogram (EMG) Muscle Tremor
              </h6>
              <p class="small text-muted mb-2"><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Somatic muscle contraction, shivering, or patient agitation generates asynchronous motor unit action potentials (<InlineEquation equation="20 - 500\\text{ Hz}"/>).</p>
              <p class="small text-secondary mb-0"><strong>Solution:</strong> Low-pass filtering (<InlineEquation equation="f_c \\approx 35 - 150\\text{ Hz}"/>) and spatial averaging across multiple diagnostic leads.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-md-6">
          <div class="card h-100 border-start border-4 border-secondary shadow-xs">
            <div class="card-body p-3">
              <h6 class="fw-bold text-secondary mb-1">
                <i class="bi bi-thermometer-half me-1"></i> Thermal & Semiconductor Noise
              </h6>
              <p class="small text-muted mb-2"><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Johnson-Nyquist thermal agitation of electrons inside electrode gels and skin resistance (<InlineEquation equation="v_n = \\sqrt{4kTR\\Delta f}"/>) plus <InlineEquation equation="1/f"/> flicker noise in op-amps.</p>
              <p class="small text-secondary mb-0"><strong>Solution:</strong> Low-noise biopotential preamplifiers (JFET/CMOS inputs), high conductive electrode gels, and restricting measurement bandwidth.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
  </SubSection>

  <SubSection title="The Instrumentation Chain: Amplification & ADC Conversion" id="SignalChain">
      <p>A fundamental lesson in medical device engineering is: <strong>Noise must be controlled and filtered before high-gain amplification and Analog-to-Digital Conversion (ADC)</strong>. To understand why, examine the typical biomedical signal acquisition pipeline:</p>

      <!-- Instrumentation Chain Diagram -->
      <div class="card shadow-sm border my-4">
        <div class="card-header bg-white border-bottom py-2 px-3 fw-bold text-primary">
          <i class="bi bi-diagram-3-fill text-warning me-2"></i> Typical Biomedical Acquisition Signal Chain
        </div>
        <div class="card-body p-3 text-center">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 760 170" class="w-100" style="max-height: 180px;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
