              <marker id="arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="5" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">
                <path d="M 0 1 L 10 5 L 0 9 z" fill="#13294b"/>
              </marker>
            </defs>

            <!-- Box 1: Patient / Electrodes -->
            <rect x="10" y="45" width="120" height="70" rx="8" fill="#e7f1ff" stroke="#0d6efd" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="70" y="75" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#0d6efd" text-anchor="middle">Patient / Skin</text>
            <text x="70" y="93" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#495057" text-anchor="middle">Electrodes (Ag/AgCl)</text>
            <text x="70" y="106" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="9" fill="#6c757d" text-anchor="middle">V_sig ~ 1 mV</text>

            <!-- Arrow 1 -->
            <line x1="130" y1="80" x2="168" y2="80" stroke="#13294b" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>
            <text x="149" y="70" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="9" fill="#dc3545" text-anchor="middle">+ Noise</text>

            <!-- Box 2: Instrumentation Amp -->
            <rect x="170" y="45" width="130" height="70" rx="8" fill="#fff3cd" stroke="#ffc107" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="235" y="75" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#856404" text-anchor="middle">Pre-Amplifier</text>
            <text x="235" y="93" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#495057" text-anchor="middle">Instrumentation Amp</text>
            <text x="235" y="106" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="9" fill="#856404" text-anchor="middle">Gain G = 1000x</text>

            <!-- Arrow 2 -->
            <line x1="300" y1="80" x2="338" y2="80" stroke="#13294b" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>

            <!-- Box 3: Analog Filter -->
            <rect x="340" y="45" width="125" height="70" rx="8" fill="#d1e7dd" stroke="#198754" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="402" y="75" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#0f5132" text-anchor="middle">Analog Filter</text>
            <text x="402" y="93" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#495057" text-anchor="middle">Bandpass + Notch</text>
            <text x="402" y="106" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="9" fill="#0f5132" text-anchor="middle">Attenuate Noise</text>

            <!-- Arrow 3 -->
            <line x1="465" y1="80" x2="503" y2="80" stroke="#13294b" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>

            <!-- Box 4: ADC -->
            <rect x="505" y="45" width="115" height="70" rx="8" fill="#e2d9f3" stroke="#6f42c1" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="562" y="75" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#432874" text-anchor="middle">ADC</text>
            <text x="562" y="93" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#495057" text-anchor="middle">Analog-to-Digital</text>
            <text x="562" y="106" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="9" fill="#432874" text-anchor="middle">16-bit, 0-3.3V</text>

            <!-- Arrow 4 -->
            <line x1="620" y1="80" x2="648" y2="80" stroke="#13294b" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>

            <!-- Box 5: DSP / Microcontroller -->
            <rect x="650" y="45" width="100" height="70" rx="8" fill="#cfe2ff" stroke="#13294b" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="700" y="75" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#13294b" text-anchor="middle">MCU / DSP</text>
            <text x="700" y="93" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#495057" text-anchor="middle">Computer</text>
            <text x="700" y="106" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="9" fill="#13294b" text-anchor="middle">Diagnostic UI</text>
          </svg>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="row g-4 my-2">
        <div class="col-md-6">
          <div class="card h-100 border-0 shadow-sm bg-light">
            <div class="card-body p-3">
              <h5 class="fw-bold text-danger d-flex align-items-center gap-2">
                <i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle-fill"></i>
                1. Op-Amp Rail Saturation (Clipping)
              </h5>
              <p class="small text-secondary">
                A biological potential like an ECG is typically only <InlineEquation equation="1\\text{ mV} = 0.001\\text{ V}"/>. To bring this signal into a usable voltage range for microcontrollers, the pre-amplifier applies massive gain, often <InlineEquation equation="G = 1,000\\times - 5,000\\times"/>.
              </p>
              <p class="small text-secondary">
                However, linear amplifiers amplify <strong>everything</strong> present at their input:
              </p>
              <DisplayEquation title="" equation="V_{\\text{out}}(t) = G \\cdot \\left[ v_{\\text{signal}}(t) + v_{\\text{noise}}(t) \\right]"/>
              <p class="small text-secondary mb-0">
                If the input has low SNR with <InlineEquation equation="5\\text{ mV}"/> of 60 Hz hum, the desired output would be <InlineEquation equation="1000 \\times 6\\text{ mV} = 6.0\\text{ V}"/>. Because modern medical embedded systems operate on <InlineEquation equation="\\pm 2.5\\text{ V}"/> or single <InlineEquation equation="+3.3\\text{ V}"/> rails, the amplifier <strong>rails and saturates</strong>. The peaks of the ECG are clipped flat against the power rails, permanently erasing diagnostic timing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-md-6">
          <div class="card h-100 border-0 shadow-sm bg-light">
            <div class="card-body p-3">
              <h5 class="fw-bold text-primary d-flex align-items-center gap-2">
                <i class="bi bi-layers-fill"></i>
                2. ADC Dynamic Range & Bit Swamping
              </h5>
              <p class="small text-secondary">
                An Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC) divides its full-scale input span (<InlineEquation equation="V_{\\text{FS}}"/>) into <InlineEquation equation="2^N"/> discrete digital steps, where <InlineEquation equation="N"/> is the bit resolution (e.g., 16 bits = 65,536 levels).
              </p>
              <p class="small text-secondary">
                The quantization voltage step is:
              </p>
              <DisplayEquation title="" equation="Q = \\frac{V_{\\text{FS}}}{2^N}"/>
              <p class="small text-secondary mb-0">
                If an un-filtered signal with massive low-frequency noise (such as a <InlineEquation equation="1.5\\text{ V}"/> baseline wander) enters the ADC, the system gain must be turned down to prevent overflow. As a result, the actual <InlineEquation equation="1\\text{ mV}"/> ECG waveform is mapped across only a few dozen quantization steps, drastically lowering the <strong>Effective Number of Bits (ENOB)</strong> and rendering fine morphological details undetectable.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
  </SubSection>

  <SubSection title="Clinical Stakes & Medical Device Constraints" id="ClinicalImpact">
      <p>In consumer audio or telecommunications, poor SNR results in static or dropped calls. In medical devices, <strong>poor SNR can be fatal</strong>:</p>

      <div class="alert alert-danger d-flex align-items-start gap-3 p-3 my-3 rounded" role="alert">
        <i class="bi bi-shield-x fs-2 text-danger flex-shrink-0"></i>
        <div>
          <h6 class="alert-heading fw-bold mb-1">Clinical Diagnostic Risks:</h6>
          <ul class="mb-0 ps-3 small">
            <li><strong>False Alarm Fatigue:</strong> Motion artifacts mimicking ventricular tachycardia cause up to 80% of ICU bedside monitor alarms to be false positives, leading healthcare providers to experience alarm desensitization.</li>
            <li><strong>Missed Ischemia / Infarction:</strong> An ST-segment elevation of merely <InlineEquation equation="0.1\\text{ mV} (1\\text{ mm})"/> is the clinical criterion for emergency coronary catheterization (STEMI). If noise amplitude exceeds <InlineEquation equation="0.1\\text{ mV}"/>, early heart attack detection fails.</li>
            <li><strong>Inappropriate Defibrillator Shock:</strong> Implantable Cardioverter Defibrillators (ICDs) that misidentify electromyogram muscle noise as ventricular fibrillation can deliver agonizing and dangerous high-energy shocks to conscious patients.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm bg-light my-3">
        <div class="card-body p-3">
          <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark d-flex align-items-center gap-2 mb-2">
            <i class="bi bi-currency-dollar text-success fs-5"></i>
            The Engineering Tradeoff: Performance vs. Cost (<InlineEquation equation="\\uparrow \\$[\\text{MedA}]"/>)
          </h6>
          <p class="small text-secondary mb-2">
            Achieving higher SNR in medical instrumentation requires escalating engineering investment: ultra-low-noise preamps, differential shielded cabling, precision active grounding (Right Leg Drive), multi-pole linear-phase analog filters, and high-resolution sigma-delta ADCs.
          </p>
          <p class="small text-secondary mb-0">
            Meeting stringent regulatory standards—such as <strong>FDA 510(k) clearances</strong>, <strong>IEC 60601-1</strong> (medical electrical safety), and <strong>IEC 60601-2-47</strong> (ambulatory ECG fidelity)—demands rigorous noise budget analysis from sensor contact to cloud dashboard.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
  </SubSection>

